Z||000000||ponya||10-15-2000||12:00 AM||||Trying to figure out why the switch inside the car does not open the rear hatch. I took off the lower panel under the steering wheel to see if the relay was working. Hit the switch and could feel the relay and hear it click. Went back to the rear and used a meter to see if I got a reading. Hit the switch and got voltage. The solenoid did nothing. Anyone know how much voltage reading I should get at the solenoid? Any info would be apprecitated.<BR>Thanks,  Bob||||reg||5
Z||000001||862M4inCA||10-15-2000||04:50 AM||||I would imagine that when you hit the switch you should get a full 12V to the solenoid. Might just be a bad one. Correct me if I'm wrong, but isn't there a way by checking resistances, to see if it's bad? I'd think that an infinate reading would indicate a bas solenoid.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||10-15-2000||06:42 AM||||You might try running a different ground to get it to work. You should have +12 volts to the solenoid.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-15-2000||11:33 AM||||The solinoid will not fire if the lid is open. It is grounded thru the latch to the locking eye.<P>to fire with the lid open, connect a jumper cable to the latch and the engine block or the - battery terminal.<P>You said you're getting power all the way to the latch. If it won't fire with the jumper above then the solinoid has failed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hank is Here||10-15-2000||02:33 PM||||Just try smacking the solenoid with a screwdriver or hard object, this is an farmers truck generally used on starters.  My soleniod quit on my 87 GT a few weeks ago, a frw hits and now it works<P>Mark Johnson||||reg||6
